from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A classical bit can hold how many definite values at a time?","options":["only one, but it keeps changing","exactly two, either 0 or 1","infinitely many at once","three, counting a blank state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A classical bit is a single definite value, 0 or 1 - see ''Why quantum computing matters''."},{"prompt":"A qubit differs from a classical bit mainly because it can...","options":["store a whole image","sit in a superposition, a blend of |0> and |1>, while it computes","move electricity faster","work without any hardware"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The headline difference is superposition during the computation - see ''Qubits, superposition, and measurement''."},{"prompt":"When you measure a qubit that is in a superposition, you get...","options":["both 0 and 1 together","a single definite 0 or 1, with some probability each","always 0","a fraction between 0 and 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Measurement collapses the blend to one definite outcome, 0 or 1."},{"prompt":"Entanglement between two qubits means that...","options":["they are physically glued together","measuring one instantly constrains what the other will show","they spin faster than normal","their wires are crossed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pair is correlated as a whole; reading one pins down the partner''s outcome."},{"prompt":"The quantum gate that flips |0> to |1> plays the role of a classical...","options":["AND gate","NOT gate","OR gate","clock signal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The X gate is the quantum NOT - see ''Quantum gates, circuits, and interference''."},{"prompt":"Shor''s algorithm is famous because it can...","options":["sort lists instantly","factor large numbers efficiently, threatening some common encryption","render video","compress files"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Efficient factoring undermines public-key schemes that rely on factoring being hard."},{"prompt":"Decoherence refers to...","options":["qubits glowing brighter","a qubit losing its quantum state through interaction with its environment","qubits cooling down too fast","adding more qubits to a chip"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Decoherence is the central fragility problem - see ''Decoherence, hardware, and real-world prospects''."},{"prompt":"Grover''s algorithm gives roughly what kind of speedup for unstructured search?","options":["no speedup at all","a quadratic speedup","an exponential speedup in every case","it makes search slower"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Grover''s search runs in about the square root of the number of items - a quadratic gain."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Why quantum computing matters, and how it differs from classical computing', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain in plain words what a quantum computer is, why people are building one, and how it departs from the ordinary computer on your desk. By the end of this lesson you will be able to name the single physical idea - storing information in quantum systems - that makes the whole field different, and you will be able to say honestly what a quantum machine is good for and, just as important, what it is not. You will also be ready to push back on the hype you often hear, because you will understand that a quantum computer is not merely a quicker version of your laptop. Warm-up: think of one task your laptop does well, such as sending an email or editing a photo, and ask yourself whether raw speed is really the thing that would change if the underlying hardware obeyed a different set of physical rules, or whether the machine you already own is simply the right tool for that job."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A classical computer stores everything as bits, each one a definite 0 or 1, and it processes them with logic gates built from transistors. This model is astonishingly successful, and for the vast majority of jobs it is exactly what you want. A quantum computer instead keeps information in quantum systems - tiny objects such as atoms, ions, or superconducting circuits - that follow the rules of quantum mechanics rather than everyday intuition. The headline difference is that a quantum bit need not be a plain 0 or 1 while it is computing; it can occupy a blend of both at once, and several quantum bits can be linked so that their possibilities are woven together. This does not mean the machine tries every answer in parallel like a magic box. Instead it manipulates the blended states so that, when you finally measure, the wrong answers tend to cancel out and the right ones stand out. For a few special problems this buys an enormous advantage; for most ordinary problems it buys nothing at all."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Start with the pieces. A classical bit is written 0 or 1. A quantum bit, or qubit, uses the states |0> and |1>, and during a computation it can sit in a combination such as (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), which is an even blend of the two. The numbers in front of |0> and |1> are called amplitudes, and they can be positive or negative, which turns out to matter a great deal. A handful of good target problems have emerged. Simulating the behavior of molecules and materials is a natural fit, because nature itself is quantum. Factoring very large numbers is another, through Shor''s algorithm. Searching an unsorted pile of options can be sped up with Grover''s algorithm. By contrast, adding a column of numbers, serving a web page, or storing your photos gains nothing from quantum hardware - a classical chip does these perfectly. The honest summary: a quantum computer is a specialized instrument, not a universal replacement for the machine you already own."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In one sentence, what does a qubit let you do that a bit cannot? 2) True or false: a quantum computer simply tries all answers at the same time. 3) Name one problem well suited to quantum hardware. 4) Name one everyday task that gains nothing from it. 5) What are the numbers in front of |0> and |1> called? Answers: 1) It can hold a blend, or superposition, of 0 and 1 while computing, rather than a single fixed value. 2) False - it shapes the blended states so that correct answers reinforce and wrong ones cancel, which is not the same as checking every answer for free. 3) Simulating molecules and materials, or factoring large numbers, or unstructured search. 4) Routine jobs such as spreadsheets, email, or storing photos, which classical hardware already handles well. 5) Amplitudes - and they can be negative, which later makes interference possible."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: believing a quantum computer is just a faster classical computer - it is a different kind of machine that helps only on particular problems. Mistake: picturing superposition as the computer secretly running every possibility in separate worlds and reading off the best; the real mechanism depends on amplitudes reinforcing and canceling, which we will build up carefully. Mistake: assuming quantum speedups apply everywhere; most tasks see no benefit, and for many the overhead would make quantum hardware slower. Exit: (a) Fill in the blank: a qubit can hold a ___ of |0> and |1> during a computation. (b) Does running a spreadsheet faster motivate quantum computing? Answers: a blend, also called a superposition; no - spreadsheets run perfectly on classical hardware and gain nothing from quantum effects, so they are not a reason to build these machines."}]}'::jsonb, 'A quantum computer is not simply a faster classical computer - it is a different kind of machine that helps only on particular problems.', 13),
  (3, 'Qubits, superposition, and measurement', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe exactly what a qubit is, write a simple superposition in plain ASCII notation, and predict the probabilities you would see when you measure it. You will be able to explain why a qubit holds more structure than a bit while it computes, yet gives back only a single 0 or 1 when you look at it. You will also be able to connect the amplitudes in a state to the chances of each measurement outcome, using the rule that you square an amplitude to get its probability. Warm-up: if a coin were spinning in the air, you might say it is neither heads nor tails yet; a qubit in superposition is loosely like that, except that the rules for what happens when it lands are completely precise rather than vague. Ask yourself what number, somewhere between 0 and 1, you would expect for the chance of seeing 0 when a qubit is an equal blend of |0> and |1>, and whether that guess matches a fair coin."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A qubit has two basic states, written |0> and |1>, the quantum versions of the classical 0 and 1. While it is computing it can be in a superposition, a weighted combination a|0> + b|1>, where a and b are amplitudes. The amplitudes carry the quantum information. The crucial measurement rule is this: when you measure the qubit in the standard basis, you never see a blend. You see 0 with probability equal to a times a, and 1 with probability equal to b times b, and the state then collapses to whichever outcome you got. Because the total probability must be 1, the amplitudes always satisfy a times a plus b times b equals 1. So a state like (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) has amplitude 1/sqrt(2) on each side, and squaring gives 1/2 for each outcome - a fair coin. Superposition is therefore not vagueness; it is a precise description of the odds and, as we will see later, of how different computational paths can interfere before you ever measure."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Take three states and read off their behavior. First, |0> by itself: amplitude 1 on |0> and 0 on |1>, so you measure 0 with certainty. Second, the equal blend (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2): each amplitude is 1/sqrt(2), each squares to 1/2, so you get 0 half the time and 1 half the time. Third, a lopsided blend such as sqrt(0.9)|0> + sqrt(0.1)|1>: squaring the amplitudes gives 0.9 and 0.1, so 0 appears ninety percent of the time. Notice a subtle point: amplitudes can be negative. The state (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2) gives the same 50-50 measurement odds as (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), because squaring removes the sign. Yet that minus sign is not harmless - it changes how the state behaves when you apply further operations, which is the seed of interference. Measurement also destroys the superposition: once you have seen 0, repeated measurements keep giving 0, because the state collapsed to |0> and stays there."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) For the state (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), what is the chance of measuring 1? 2) For sqrt(0.25)|0> + sqrt(0.75)|1>, what are the two outcome probabilities? 3) Do (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) and (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2) give different measurement odds? 4) After you measure a qubit and see 0, what do you get if you immediately measure it again? 5) Why must a times a plus b times b equal 1? Answers: 1) (1/sqrt(2)) squared equals 1/2, so fifty percent. 2) 0.25 for outcome 0 and 0.75 for outcome 1. 3) No - both give 50-50, since squaring hides the sign; the difference shows up only under further operations such as gates. 4) 0 again, because the first measurement collapsed the state to |0>. 5) Because the probabilities of the only two possible outcomes must add up to 1."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a measured qubit still holds its blend - measurement collapses it to a single definite value. Mistake: reading amplitudes as probabilities directly; you must square them, and an amplitude can be negative while a probability cannot. Mistake: assuming the sign of an amplitude never matters - it does not change one measurement''s odds, but it steers interference later. Exit: (a) If a qubit is (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), what is the probability of measuring 0? (b) True or false: amplitudes and probabilities are the same thing. Answers: 1/2, because 1/sqrt(2) squared is 1/2; false - you square an amplitude to get a probability, and amplitudes may be negative whereas probabilities always lie between 0 and 1."}]}'::jsonb, 'Measurement is not a passive look - it forces a qubit in superposition to settle on a single definite outcome, 0 or 1, and then the blend is gone.', 14),
  (4, 'The Bloch sphere and quantum states', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Picture a single qubit as a point on the surface of a sphere, name what the poles and the equator stand for, and use that picture to reason about states and about simple operations on them. By the end you will be able to place the states |0>, |1>, and the equal blends in their correct spots, and to describe a quantum gate as a rotation of the sphere rather than as a wall of symbols. This visual intuition is valuable because the algebra of amplitudes, while exact, can hide what is really going on. It also has honest limits, which you will be able to state: the sphere describes one qubit only, and it does not extend in any simple way to two or more. Warm-up: if |0> sits at the north pole and |1> sits at the south pole, where would you expect an even fifty-fifty blend of the two to live - crowded near a pole, or somewhere around the equator in the middle?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Any single-qubit state can be drawn as a point on a sphere of radius one, called the Bloch sphere. The north pole is |0> and the south pole is |1>. Every other point on the surface is a particular superposition. Points around the equator are equal fifty-fifty blends of |0> and |1>, but they differ from one another in a feature called phase - roughly, the relative sign or angle between the two amplitudes. For example, (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) and (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2) both sit on the equator, on opposite sides, because they share the same 50-50 odds but carry opposite phase. Moving from a pole toward the equator increases how mixed the state is. The great value of this picture is that operations on a qubit become rotations of the sphere: every single-qubit gate simply turns the point to a new position on the surface, and undoing a gate is just rotating it back the other way."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Locate some landmarks. The north pole is |0>, measured as 0 with certainty; the south pole is |1>. The point facing you on the equator is (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), often labeled |+>, and the point directly behind it is (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2), labeled |->. These two sit opposite each other precisely because the minus sign is a half-turn of phase. Now read gates as motions. The gate that swaps |0> and |1> flips the sphere top to bottom, carrying the north pole to the south pole. The gate that creates an even blend tips the north pole down to the equator, turning |0> into |+>. A pure phase change spins the point around the vertical axis without moving it off its latitude, which is why it leaves measurement odds in the standard basis untouched while still mattering for interference. Distance from a pole corresponds to how balanced the measurement odds are: at a pole they are certain, and at the equator they are an even fifty-fifty."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which state sits at the north pole? 2) Where on the sphere do all the fifty-fifty blends live? 3) The states |+> and |-> are on opposite sides of the equator - what feature distinguishes them? 4) If a gate carries the north pole to the south pole, what does it do to |0>? 5) Does a pure phase spin change the standard measurement odds? Answers: 1) |0>. 2) On the equator, since equal blends are the most mixed states. 3) Their phase - the relative sign between the amplitudes, a minus versus a plus. 4) It sends |0> to |1>, that is, it flips the qubit. 5) No - it keeps the point at the same latitude, so the squared amplitudes, and thus the odds, stay the same, though the phase it changes can still matter for later interference."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: trying to draw two qubits on one Bloch sphere - the picture works for a single qubit only, and an entangled pair has no such simple point. Mistake: treating two equatorial states as identical because they share the same measurement odds; their phase, their position around the equator, sets them apart. Mistake: thinking a phase change does nothing because it does not alter standard measurement odds - it quietly prepares the interference that algorithms rely on. Exit: (a) What states sit at the two poles of the Bloch sphere? (b) What does an equatorial position tell you about the measurement odds? Answers: |0> at the north pole and |1> at the south pole; an equatorial state is a fair fifty-fifty blend, equally likely to give 0 or 1 when measured."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Bloch sphere is a picture of one qubit only; it does not extend in any simple way to two or more qubits.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A qubit differs from a classical bit because it can...","options":["be copied freely","hold a superposition of |0> and |1> while computing","run without power","store an entire document"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining feature is superposition during the computation."},{"prompt":"For the state (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), the probability of measuring 0 is...","options":["1","1/2","1/sqrt(2)","0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Square the amplitude 1/sqrt(2) to get 1/2."},{"prompt":"Measuring a qubit that is in a superposition...","options":["leaves it unchanged","collapses it to a single definite 0 or 1","always returns 0","returns a fraction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Measurement collapses the blend to one outcome, and the superposition is gone."},{"prompt":"On the Bloch sphere, the state |0> sits at...","options":["the equator","the north pole","the south pole","the center"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By convention |0> is the north pole and |1> is the south pole."},{"prompt":"Two equatorial states with the same measurement odds can still differ in their...","options":["mass","phase","temperature","length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Phase is the relative sign or angle between the amplitudes, as with |+> and |->."},{"prompt":"To turn an amplitude into a probability, you...","options":["add 1 to it","square it","double it","take only its sign"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The probability of an outcome is the square of its amplitude."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Entanglement', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define entanglement carefully, recognize the most famous entangled two-qubit state, and explain why measuring one qubit can instantly pin down what a partner qubit will show, even though nothing is sent between them. You will be able to separate the real physics from the science-fiction version, and to state plainly why entanglement does not allow anyone to send a message faster than light. Most importantly, you will be able to say where the information actually lives: in the pair taken as a whole, not in either qubit on its own. Warm-up: imagine two coins prepared so that whenever one shows heads the other also shows heads, and whenever one shows tails the other shows tails, every single time, no matter how far apart you carry them before flipping. What would you need to know about the pair, beyond a description of each coin by itself, to capture that linked behavior - and could you use it to send a note?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Two qubits can be placed in a joint state that cannot be written as one qubit''s state alongside another''s. That is entanglement: the pair has a definite combined description, yet neither member has a state of its own. The classic example is the pair (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2). Read the labels: |00> means both qubits are 0, and |11> means both are 1. This state says the two will always be found to agree - both 0 or both 1 - with equal odds for each case, and never one 0 and the other 1. Before measurement neither qubit is secretly 0 or 1; the correlation is all there is. When you measure the first qubit and happen to see 0, the state collapses so the second is now certain to read 0 as well, and the same holds for 1. Nothing physical passes from one to the other; what changes is your description of a pair that was always correlated. This linkage, not raw parallelism, is a key resource behind many quantum algorithms."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Work through the famous state (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2). Square the amplitudes: a 1/2 chance of outcome 00 and a 1/2 chance of 11, with zero chance of 01 or 10. So the two qubits are perfectly correlated. Suppose you hold the first qubit and a friend carries the second to another city. You measure and see 1; instantly the joint description says your friend''s qubit will read 1 when they measure. Did you send them a signal? No - your friend sees only a random-looking 0 or 1 and cannot tell that you measured at all until you compare notes over an ordinary phone line, which travels no faster than light. The surprising part is only that the outcomes were coordinated without either qubit holding a private value in advance. Compare an ordinary unentangled pair, such as |0> for the first and (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) for the second: here each qubit has its own state, the second is a fair coin regardless of the first, and measuring one tells you nothing about the other."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In the state (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2), what is the chance of measuring 01? 2) If you measure the first qubit of that state and get 1, what will the second qubit give? 3) Can entanglement be used to send a message faster than light? 4) Where does the information in an entangled pair reside? 5) True or false: before measurement each qubit of the pair already holds a secret 0 or 1. Answers: 1) Zero - only 00 and 11 ever appear. 2) 1, with certainty, because the outcomes are perfectly correlated. 3) No - each party sees random results and must compare notes through an ordinary channel to notice the correlation. 4) In the pair as a whole, not in either qubit alone. 5) False - neither qubit has a definite value in advance; the correlation is the whole story."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining a hidden wire or signal passing between entangled qubits - there is none, and no information travels. Mistake: thinking entanglement lets you communicate instantly; the local results look random, so no message gets through faster than an ordinary channel allows. Mistake: describing each entangled qubit with its own separate state - the defining feature is that you cannot, because only the pair has a description. Exit: (a) In (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2), which two outcomes are possible? (b) If the first qubit reads 0, what does the second read? Answers: 00 and 11, each with probability one half; the second reads 0 as well, because the pair is perfectly correlated."}]}'::jsonb, 'Entanglement is a property of a group of qubits taken together; it cannot be described by looking at each qubit on its own.', 13),
  (7, 'Quantum gates, circuits, and interference', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read a small quantum circuit, say what the X, H, and CNOT gates each do, and explain how interference - the reinforcing and canceling of amplitudes - is the true engine behind quantum speedups. You will be able to trace a single qubit through a gate step by step, to combine two qubits with a controlled gate, and to see why a quantum program is really a sequence of reversible operations that ends in a measurement. You will also be able to explain, without hand-waving, why negative amplitudes are the ingredient that makes interference possible in the first place. Warm-up: classical logic has a NOT gate that flips 0 to 1 and 1 to 0. If a quantum gate also flips |0> and |1>, what do you expect to happen if you apply that same flip twice in a row to the same qubit, and does that fit the idea that quantum gates can always be undone?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A quantum circuit is a line of qubits, drawn as wires, with gates applied in order and measurements at the end. Three gates cover most of the intuition. The X gate is the quantum NOT: it sends |0> to |1> and |1> to |0>. The H gate, or Hadamard, builds superposition: it turns |0> into (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) and turns |1> into (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2), where that minus sign is deliberate and important. The CNOT gate acts on two qubits: it flips the second qubit only when the first is |1>, which is how circuits create entanglement. Every quantum gate is reversible, so applying X twice returns the original state, and applying H twice does too. The deep point is interference. Because amplitudes can be positive or negative, the many paths a computation takes can add up where you want the answer and cancel where you do not. Shaping that cancellation is exactly what a good quantum algorithm is for."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Trace a few circuits. Apply X to |0>: you get |1>. Apply X again: back to |0>, confirming reversibility. Apply H to |0>: you get (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), a fair coin. Now the key demonstration of interference: apply H a second time to that result. The two paths leading to |0> both carry positive amplitude and reinforce, while the two paths leading to |1> carry opposite signs and cancel exactly, so you recover |0> with certainty - not a coin at all. That cancellation is interference in its purest form, and it is only possible because one amplitude was negative. For entanglement, start with the first qubit as |0>, apply H to make (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), then apply CNOT with it controlling a second qubit that began at |0>: the result is (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2), the entangled pair from the previous lesson. A handful of gates, chosen well, thus produce superposition, entanglement, and interference in turn."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the X gate do to |1>? 2) What state does H produce from |0>? 3) If you apply H twice to |0>, what do you get, and why? 4) What does CNOT do to its second qubit? 5) Which feature of amplitudes makes interference possible? Answers: 1) It sends |1> to |0>, the quantum NOT. 2) (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2), an equal superposition. 3) You get |0> back with certainty, because the paths to |1> cancel and the paths to |0> reinforce - that is interference. 4) It flips the second qubit exactly when the first qubit is |1>, and otherwise leaves it alone. 5) That amplitudes can be negative as well as positive, so contributions can cancel, not only add."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating a quantum gate like a classical one-way gate - every quantum gate is reversible, so there is no direct quantum AND that throws information away. Mistake: forgetting the minus sign that H puts on |1>; drop it and interference disappears, and the whole advantage goes with it. Mistake: thinking CNOT always flips its target - it flips only when the control qubit is |1>. Exit: (a) Applying X twice to any qubit gives you what? (b) In the double-H on |0>, why do you end up back at |0>? Answers: the original state, since X is its own reverse; because the amplitudes on the |1> paths cancel while those on the |0> paths add, a clean case of interference."}]}'::jsonb, 'A quantum gate must be reversible - every gate can be undone - which is why there is no quantum version of a one-way gate like classical AND.', 14),
  (8, 'Famous algorithms: Deutsch, Grover, and Shor', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe, at a high level, three landmark quantum algorithms and what each one buys you: the Deutsch algorithm as the first clean proof that a quantum approach can beat a classical one on a toy task, Grover''s search for a quadratic speedup on unstructured search, and Shor''s factoring for an exponential speedup that threatens widely used encryption. You will be able to state what problem each one solves and roughly how much faster it is, without getting lost in the mathematics. Warm-up: if checking one option in a list of a million takes a single step, about how many steps would a plain classical search need in the worst case, and would cutting that number in half, or down to its square root, feel significant to you when the list grows to a billion items?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"These three algorithms show the range of quantum advantage. The Deutsch algorithm, later extended by Deutsch and Jozsa, answers a deliberately simple question - is a hidden function constant or balanced - using a single evaluation where a classical approach may need two or more. Its value is not practical but conceptual: it was an early, clean demonstration that interference can do real work. Grover''s algorithm searches an unstructured list of N items for a marked one in about the square root of N steps, compared with roughly N steps classically. That is a quadratic speedup: for a million items, near a thousand steps instead of a million. Shor''s algorithm factors a large number into its prime parts in time that grows only polynomially with the number of digits, whereas the best known classical methods grow far faster, effectively exponentially. Because the security of common public-key encryption rests on factoring being hard, Shor''s result is the single most cited reason that a large, reliable quantum computer would matter - and would force a change in cryptography."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Put numbers to the speedups. Suppose you must find one marked entry among a million unsorted entries. Classically you expect to check about half a million, and up to a million in the worst case. Grover''s algorithm finds it in roughly the square root of a million, which is about a thousand iterations - dramatic, but only a quadratic gain, so it does not break encryption by itself. Now take factoring. To factor a number with hundreds of digits, the best classical methods would run for longer than the age of the universe on any machine we can build; Shor''s algorithm, on a large enough error-corrected quantum computer, would finish in a manageable time, because its cost grows roughly with the cube of the digit count rather than exploding. The Deutsch example is smaller but instructive: given a one-bit function hidden in a box, one quantum query plus interference reveals a global property that classically needs two queries. Each case trades brute force for cleverly arranged cancellation."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which algorithm gives a quadratic speedup for unstructured search? 2) About how many steps does Grover''s search need for a million items? 3) Which algorithm threatens public-key encryption, and why? 4) What question does the Deutsch algorithm answer? 5) Does Grover''s quadratic speedup break common encryption on its own? Answers: 1) Grover''s algorithm. 2) Roughly the square root of a million, about one thousand. 3) Shor''s algorithm, because it factors large numbers efficiently and common public-key schemes rely on factoring being hard. 4) Whether a hidden function is constant or balanced, using very few evaluations. 5) No - a quadratic gain is not enough to defeat well-chosen key sizes, so it mainly motivates somewhat larger keys rather than a full redesign."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming every quantum algorithm gives an exponential speedup - most give modest or no gains, and Grover''s is only quadratic. Mistake: believing these algorithms already run usefully today; they need far more reliable qubits than current machines provide. Mistake: confusing Grover and Shor - Grover speeds up search quadratically, while Shor factors numbers and is the real threat to encryption. That difference is why cryptographers worry about Shor far more than about Grover. Exit: (a) Which algorithm factors large numbers efficiently? (b) What size of speedup does Grover''s algorithm provide? Answers: Shor''s algorithm; a quadratic speedup, turning about N steps into about the square root of N."}]}'::jsonb, 'A quantum speedup is a claim about how the running time grows with problem size, not a promise that every instance runs faster on today''s hardware.', 13),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"qubit","translation":"the basic unit of quantum information, able to hold a blend of |0> and |1> while computing","example":"A qubit in (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2) is equally likely to read 0 or 1 when measured."},{"term":"superposition","translation":"a weighted combination of |0> and |1> that a qubit occupies before measurement","example":"The H gate turns |0> into the superposition (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2)."},{"term":"amplitude","translation":"a number, possibly negative, in front of |0> or |1> whose square gives an outcome''s probability","example":"In (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2) each amplitude has size 1/sqrt(2), so each outcome has probability 1/2."},{"term":"measurement","translation":"the act of reading a qubit, which collapses any superposition to a single definite 0 or 1","example":"Measuring an equal blend gives 0 or 1 with equal chance, and the blend is then gone."},{"term":"entanglement","translation":"a link among qubits so that the group has a joint state no single qubit has on its own","example":"The pair (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2) is entangled and always agrees, both 0 or both 1."},{"term":"interference","translation":"the reinforcing and canceling of amplitudes that powers quantum algorithms","example":"Applying two Hadamards to |0> cancels the |1> paths through interference and returns |0>."},{"term":"decoherence","translation":"the loss of a qubit''s quantum state through unwanted interaction with its environment","example":"Cold temperatures and careful shielding slow decoherence in real hardware."},{"term":"quantum gate","translation":"a reversible operation that transforms qubit states, such as X, H, or CNOT","example":"The X gate acts as a quantum NOT, sending |0> to |1> and back again."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (10, 'Decoherence, hardware, and real-world prospects', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why quantum states are so fragile, name two leading hardware approaches, and give a sober account of what quantum computers can and cannot be expected to do in the coming years, including their effect on cryptography. You will be able to define decoherence, describe the idea of quantum error correction without the heavy mathematics, and separate realistic prospects from marketing. Warm-up: a soap bubble holds its delicate shape until the slightest touch pops it, and quantum states are similar in their fragility. What everyday disturbances - heat, stray electromagnetic signals, tiny vibrations - do you think a machine holding such states would need to be shielded from, and why might keeping a qubit still and cold be half the battle in building a useful computer?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Quantum advantages depend on keeping delicate superposition and entanglement intact, but real qubits constantly leak their quantum character into the surrounding world. This leakage is called decoherence, and together with imperfect gates it is the central engineering obstacle. The response is quantum error correction: encode one protected logical qubit across many physical qubits, so that errors can be detected and reversed before they ruin the computation. The cost is steep - a single logical qubit may demand hundreds or thousands of physical ones - which is why useful machines are hard to build. On the hardware side, two approaches lead. Superconducting qubits are tiny circuits cooled to near absolute zero and controlled with microwave pulses; they are fast but short-lived. Trapped-ion qubits are individual charged atoms held in electromagnetic fields and nudged with lasers; they last longer and are very accurate but operate more slowly. Both are being scaled up, and neither has clearly won. The field is real, serious, and still early."},{"heading":"Key concepts & examples","text":"Make the trade-offs concrete. A superconducting qubit may hold its state for only millionths of a second, yet perform gates in billionths of a second, so a circuit must be short and fast; the whole chip sits in a refrigerator colder than deep space to suppress thermal noise. A trapped-ion qubit can hold its state far longer and perform gates with very low error, but each operation takes more time, and wiring many ions together is its own challenge. Error correction ties these together: if physical qubits fail, say, one time in a thousand, you can combine many of them so the encoded logical qubit fails far less often - but only once the physical error rate drops below a threshold, which today''s machines are only beginning to reach. On cryptography, the practical message is twofold. A large error-corrected machine running Shor''s algorithm could break today''s common public-key encryption, so the world is already moving to post-quantum schemes designed to resist it. But that machine does not exist yet, and building it remains hard."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is decoherence? 2) Why does error correction need many physical qubits per logical qubit? 3) Name the two leading hardware approaches described here. 4) Which approach is fast but short-lived, and which is slower but longer-lived? 5) What is the practical response to Shor''s threat against encryption? Answers: 1) The loss of a qubit''s quantum state as it interacts with its environment. 2) Because one protected logical qubit is encoded across many physical ones so that errors can be caught and reversed. 3) Superconducting circuits and trapped ions. 4) Superconducting qubits are fast but short-lived; trapped ions are slower but longer-lived and very accurate. 5) Moving to post-quantum encryption designed to resist quantum attacks, before a large machine is built."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming a logical qubit is one device - it is a protected pattern spread across many physical qubits. Mistake: believing a quantum computer large enough to break encryption already exists; the hardware is improving but is not there yet. Mistake: thinking qubits fail only from obvious mistakes - the quiet, constant leak of decoherence is the harder enemy. Mistake: imagining that more qubits alone bring progress; low error rates and correction matter at least as much. Exit: (a) What word names the loss of a qubit''s quantum state to its environment? (b) Why does breaking today''s encryption require error correction? Answers: decoherence; because Shor''s algorithm needs a large, reliable computation, and only error correction can keep many fragile qubits coherent long enough to finish it."}]}'::jsonb, 'Error correction spreads one logical qubit across many physical qubits; a ''logical qubit'' is not a single device but a protected pattern.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-000000000038' and l.position = v.pos
);
